  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/aspredinidae/bunocephalus_knerii.htm",
      "html": "<p>South America: Upper Amazon River basin of Colombia, Ecuador, and Peru. Type locality: Canelos, Ecuador.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/aspredinidae/bunocephalus_knerii.htm",
      "html": "<p>Very peaceful species which will do well in a quiet community tank where the tank mates are non-aggressive. Hides in the sand or amongst plants. Provide a sand substrate due to its habit and a planted dark area to make them feel more secure in their surroundings.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/aspredinidae/bunocephalus_knerii.htm",
      "html": "<p>Tablet foods, insect larvae and tubifex.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/aspredinidae/bunocephalus_knerii.htm",
      "html": "<p>Etymology: The genus name Bunocephalus: buno-, mound or knob; cephalus, head, referring to rounded cusps or bumps on the head. The specific name knerii: named in honour of Dr. Rudolf Kner, the Austrian ichthyologist.</p><p>IUCN Red List: This species is assessed as Least Concern due to its large distribution and the lack of major threats. Further research is needed into the taxonomy of this species (IUCN 2021).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/aspredinidae/bunocephalus_knerii.htm",
      "html": "<p>Baensch, H.A. and R. Riehl 1985 Aquarien atlas. Band 2. Mergus, Verlag für Natur- und Heimtierkunde GmbH, Melle, Germany. 1216 p.</p><p>Frederico, R.G., Reis, R. &amp; Lima, F. 2021. Bunocephalus knerii. The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species 2021.</p>"
    }
